{0 Vault operations}

Vault operations are library services shared by protocol and command-line
clients. They consume a vault snapshot and return values or a rename plan;
they do not write files or construct LSP messages.

{1 Links and graph queries}

{!Oystermark.Vault.Index.Note.links} collects authored link occurrences in a
note. {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve} resolves each occurrence against an
index snapshot. Together they provide the primitives used by vault clients.

The command-line graph statistics count Markdown notes as nodes and resolved
link occurrences as edges. Each authored occurrence is one edge, including
embeds. An edge whose resolved destination is its source note is
also counted as a self link. Unresolved occurrences are reported separately
and are not edges.

Queries must run on documents before embed expansion. Expanded embeds repeat
another note's syntax in the host document and would therefore manufacture
links that the author did not write there.

{1 Rename}

{!Oystermark.Vault.Rename} plans note and heading renames as byte edits plus
an optional file move. A client owns application:
{ul
 {- the LSP client converts byte edits and moves to a workspace edit;}
 {- the command-line client previews the plan and writes it only when asked.}
}

Keeping application outside the library lets editor-buffer snapshots and
filesystem snapshots use the same rename semantics.

{1 Command line}

The [oystermark-vault] executable exposes the shared services:
{v
oystermark-vault unresolved VAULT
oystermark-vault stats VAULT
oystermark-vault rename-note VAULT NOTE NEW-NAME [--apply]
oystermark-vault rename-heading VAULT NOTE HEADING NEW-HEADING [--apply]
v}

Rename commands are dry runs unless [--apply] is present.
